---
// The page Google Play asks every app with accounts to have: a web address
// where a reader can find out how to delete their account and what happens
// to their data (Play's Data safety form, "Delete account URL"). Apple's
// reviewers can be pointed here too, though Apple's own rule is met inside
// the app.
//
// UNTIL ACCOUNTS OPEN it is noindex, out of the sitemap (astro.config.mjs),
// and linked only from the account page, and it says so at the top. THE
// SIGN-IN AND THE BUTTON ARE BUILT (the Study Desk's step 4h): the box under
// "How to delete your account" is the account page's own code
// (src/scripts/desk/account/page.js, loaded by src/scripts/account-gate.js),
// showing sign-in and "Delete account" and nothing else. It is drawn only
// where accounts are open in a browser (?account=preview, until launch), so
// for everyone else the page reads as before. Parts marked
// data-account-closed show while accounts are closed and parts marked
// data-account-opened once they are open (src/styles/pages/account.css); at
// launch the closed parts are deleted.
//
// WHAT PLAY'S RULE ASKS OF THIS PAGE, as its help page read on 2026-11-24
// (support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/13327111): a
// reader must be able to ASK for deletion here, without installing or
// opening the app, and the way to ask must be plain on the page. Steps
// alone don't meet it. An email address or a form would, but neither can
// work here, since the service keeps no name or address to find an account
// by. So the sign-in and button below must be open to every reader before an
// Android build that makes accounts goes to review. The page must also name the app or the
// developer as the Play listing does ("LIT Bible," "Liberating Scripture
// Collective"): keep both names in the header.
//
// Every fact here is one the notebook service does and tests
// (STUDY-DESK-ENCRYPTION.md, "Deleting the account" and "Waiting seven
// days"). Change the two together. BDR reviews it, as the one who files the
// stores' forms; the in-app steps below are BDR's to confirm for each app.
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
// The privacy page's document column, used as it is. Nothing is added to
// that stylesheet for this page.
import "../styles/pages/privacy.css";
// The box that signs in and deletes: the account page's own styles.
import "../styles/pages/account.css";
---

<Layout
  title="Delete your account | Liberation and Inclusion Translation"
  description="How to delete a LIT Bible account, what is deleted, and what stays on your devices."
  canonical="https://litbible.net/delete-account/"
  robotsNoindex={true}
>
  <article class="privacy-page" aria-labelledby="delete-title">

    <header class="privacy-header">
      <div class="privacy-header__inner">
        <h1 id="delete-title" class="privacy-title">Delete your account</h1>
        <p class="privacy-effective">
          LIT Bible, from the Liberating Scripture Collective
        </p>
        <p class="privacy-lede" data-account-opened>
          <strong>You can delete your account on this page,</strong> with no
          app installed: sign in under “How to delete your account” and
          choose “Delete account.” The rest of the page says what is
          deleted and what stays on your devices.
        </p>
        <p class="privacy-lede" data-account-closed>
          <strong>Accounts are not open yet.</strong> Today the LIT Bible
          website and the iOS and Android apps keep everything you create on
          your own device, and there is no account to delete. This page is
          ready for the day accounts open, and describes how deleting one
          will work.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="privacy-body">

      <section aria-labelledby="delete-what">
        <h2 id="delete-what">What an account is</h2>
        <p>
          A LIT Bible account keeps your notebook (your notes, highlights,
          bookmarks, and sheets) in step across your devices. The copy we
          hold is locked on your devices before it is sent, with a key we
          never have, so we can’t read it. We don’t keep your name or your
          email address with it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="delete-how">
        <h2 id="delete-how">How to delete your account</h2>
        <p>You can delete your account from any place you are signed in.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>In the LIT Bible app for iPhone and iPad:</strong> open
            Settings, choose “Account,” and then choose “Delete account.”
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>In the LIT Bible app for Android:</strong> open Settings,
            choose “Account,” and then choose “Delete account.”
          </li>
          <li data-account-closed>
            <strong>On the web:</strong> when accounts open, you will be able
            to sign in on this page and choose “Delete account” here, with no
            app installed.
          </li>
          <li data-account-opened>
            <strong>On the web:</strong> sign in here and choose “Delete
            account,” with no app installed.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <div class="account-sheet" data-account-page="delete" data-account-opened></div>
        <p>
          We can’t delete an account for you from an email or a form. Because
          we don’t keep your name or email address, we have no way to tell
          which account is yours. Only someone signed in to an account can
          delete it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="delete-when">
        <h2 id="delete-when">How long it takes</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>From a device that holds your notebook’s key,</strong>
            the account is deleted at once.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>From a device that is signed in but has not opened your
            notebook,</strong> the request waits seven days. During that
            time every device that holds your notebook shows the request,
            and any of them can cancel it. This is so that someone who gets
            into your Apple or Google account can’t delete your notebook
            before you notice.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="delete-deleted">
        <h2 id="delete-deleted">What is deleted</h2>
        <p>Everything we hold for the account:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>the synced copy of your notebook, with every note, highlight, bookmark, and sheet in it;</li>
          <li>the locked copies of your notebook’s key, your recovery code’s included;</li>
          <li>the list of your devices;</li>
          <li>your ways to sign in (the link to your Apple or Google account, and your passkey’s public half);</li>
          <li>every signed-in session, on every device.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          If you signed in with Apple, we also tell Apple to end the
          connection between your Apple Account and LIT Bible.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="delete-kept">
        <h2 id="delete-kept">What is not deleted</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>The notebook on each of your devices.</strong> Deleting
            your account removes the copy we hold. Each device keeps its own
            notebook until you erase it there.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Your passkey.</strong> It lives in your password manager
            or on your device, where we can’t reach it. After your account
            is deleted it opens nothing, and you can remove it there.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Backups, for up to 30 days.</strong> The service that
            runs our database keeps a history of it for up to 30 days, so
            that we can recover from a failure. Your deleted account can
            remain in that history until it ages out. Your notebook in it is
            still locked with a key we never have.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="delete-after">
        <h2 id="delete-after">Afterward</h2>
        <p>
          Deleting an account can’t be undone. If you sign in again later
          with the same Apple or Google account, you start a new, empty
          account. Your devices still have their own notebooks, and you can
          send one of them up to the new account.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="delete-help">
        <h2 id="delete-help">Questions</h2>
        <p>
          For help, write to the LIT Bible team through
          <a href="/app-support/">app support</a> or the
          <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>. Our
          <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> describes what the website
          and the apps collect, and our <a href="/terms/">Terms of Use</a>
          are the agreement for using them.
        </p>
      </section>

    </div>
  </article>
  <script>
    import "../scripts/account-gate.js";
  </script>
</Layout>
